            <ArticleTitle>Abdominal aortitis on PET CT: A case report and review of the literature.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>104-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ijscr.2015.02.029</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S2210-2612(15)00093-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Aortitis often occurs in patients with systemic vasculitis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PRESENTATION OF CASE" NlmCategory="METHODS">We reported a 73 year old man with giant cell arteritis who was presented with abdominal pain and weight loss.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Aortitis was diagnosed on PET-CT scan performed because initial investigations raised the possibility of pancreatic pathology.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This case highlights the utility of PET-CT in the diagnosis of abdominal aortitis and the need to consider aortitis as a differential in patients with abdominal pain with a history of vasculitis.</AbstractText>
